TITLE:: PltMarkov
summary:: A transition matrix as a heat map, with its stationary distribution
categories:: Libraries>PlotLib
related:: Overviews/PlotLib, Classes/PltDistribution

DESCRIPTION::
Rows are the state moved from, columns the state moved to. Probability is
brightness, the stationary distribution runs under the columns, and each row's
entropy in bits sits beside it.

A matrix is unreadable as numbers and obvious as a picture: where a chain gets
stuck, which states are dead ends, and how much freedom each one has.

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: new
argument:: matrix
Rows of weights, normalised on the way in, so counts work as well as probabilities.
argument:: labels
argument:: title
argument:: width
argument:: height

METHOD:: fromSequence
Counted from a sequence of symbols: what a generated passage actually did, rather
than what its rules said.
argument:: symbols
argument:: states
Fixes the order of the axes. Left out, the symbols in order of first appearance.
argument:: title
argument:: width
argument:: height

METHOD:: count
Transition counts from a sequence, normalised.

METHOD:: statesOf
The distinct symbols, in order of first appearance.

METHOD:: normalise
Rows summed to 1. A row of zeros is left alone: it is a dead end, and spreading it
into a uniform row would invent transitions that never happened.

METHOD:: stationary
Where the chain spends its time in the long run, by power iteration from a uniform
start.
code::
PltMarkov.stationary([[0.9, 0.1], [0.5, 0.5]]);   // [0.8333, 0.1667]
::

METHOD:: entropy
Each row in bits: 0 when the next state is certain, code::log2(n):: when it is a
free choice.
code::
PltMarkov.entropy([[1, 0], [0.5, 0.5]]);          // [0, 1]
::

INSTANCEMETHODS::

METHOD:: matrix
METHOD:: labels
METHOD:: stationary
METHOD:: entropies
METHOD:: showValues
Print the probability in each cell, when the cells are wide enough.
METHOD:: gamma
Brightness curve of the heat map.
METHOD:: stripHeight
Height of the stationary bars.

EXAMPLES::
code::
// a drum chain: snare usually follows kick, rest returns to kick
(
PltMarkov([
	[0.1, 0.7, 0.2, 0.0],
	[0.3, 0.1, 0.4, 0.2],
	[0.0, 0.2, 0.5, 0.3],
	[0.6, 0.0, 0.0, 0.4]
], ["kick", "snare", "hat", "rest"], "four state chain").front;
)

// counted from a sequence
(
PltMarkov.fromSequence(
	[\a, \b, \c, \b, \a, \c, \c, \b, \a, \a, \b, \c, \a, \b, \b, \c, \a]
).front;
)

// what a pattern actually produced, as opposed to what it was told to do
(
~played = Pseq([0, 2, 4, 7, 4, 2], 4).asStream.all;
PltMarkov.fromSequence(~played, nil, "degrees played").front;
)

// data only
PltMarkov.normalise([[9, 1], [5, 5]]);
::
